1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,476
Senior Angular interview preparation, lesson 3.

2
00:00:03,476 --> 00:00:07,532
Accessible Angular Components: Names, Focus, and Interaction.

3
00:00:09,740 --> 00:00:11,258
Accessibility is observable behavior

4
00:00:12,382 --> 00:00:14,279
Welcome to the accessibility lesson.

5
00:00:14,279 --> 00:00:18,073
We will examine an icon button, a modal dialog, tabs,

6
00:00:18,073 --> 00:00:22,245
a form field, toast announcements, and a complete listbox coding exercise.

7
00:00:22,245 --> 00:00:26,038
Accessibility is part of a component's behavior and public contract.

8
00:00:26,038 --> 00:00:29,832
A control must have a discoverable role, a meaningful name,

9
00:00:29,832 --> 00:00:34,004
appropriate states, and an interaction model that works without a pointer.

10
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:37,322
Accessibility is observable behavior · 2

11
00:00:38,447 --> 00:00:42,006
A screenshot cannot tell you whether pressing Tab reaches the control,

12
00:00:42,006 --> 00:00:46,213
whether the focused item is selected, or whether an error is connected to

13
00:00:46,213 --> 00:00:46,860
its input.

14
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:51,066
A component can look correct while its accessibility tree tells a different story.

15
00:00:51,066 --> 00:00:53,655
Start a review by inspecting the native element,

16
00:00:53,655 --> 00:00:55,920
then its computed role and accessible name,

17
00:00:55,920 --> 00:00:57,538
then its states and relationships.

18
00:00:57,538 --> 00:01:01,745
Finally use the keyboard to operate it in the order a user would.

19
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:05,411
Accessibility is observable behavior · 3

20
00:01:06,536 --> 00:01:08,510
Our examples are teaching implementations.

21
00:01:08,510 --> 00:01:11,275
We will verify DOM relationships, keyboard events,

22
00:01:11,275 --> 00:01:14,435
native dialog behavior, and focus in a real

23
00:01:14,435 --> 00:01:14,830
browser.

24
00:01:14,830 --> 00:01:19,965
We will not treat those checks as proof of every screen reader's announcements.

25
00:01:19,965 --> 00:01:23,520
Speech output depends on browser and assistive technology combinations.

26
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:26,680
A senior answer states which behavior was tested

27
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:29,445
and where manual testing is still needed.

28
00:01:30,819 --> 00:01:32,938
Choose native semantics before adding ARIA

29
00:01:34,063 --> 00:01:39,009
A native button already supports keyboard activation, focus, disabled behavior, and a button role.

30
00:01:39,009 --> 00:01:42,188
A clickable div has none of those properties automatically.

31
00:01:42,188 --> 00:01:46,780
Adding role button only changes its semantic description; it does not make Space

32
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:51,372
activate it, put it in the tab order, or prevent activation while disabled.

33
00:01:51,372 --> 00:01:54,905
Prefer the native element when it provides the required behavior.

34
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:59,268
Choose native semantics before adding ARIA · 2

35
00:02:00,393 --> 00:02:05,247
ARIA supplements semantics when a native element does not express the complete widget.

36
00:02:05,247 --> 00:02:07,487
It describes roles, states, and relationships.

37
00:02:07,487 --> 00:02:11,595
The application remains responsible for keeping those states synchronized with behavior.

38
00:02:11,595 --> 00:02:14,956
A tab marked selected must show the corresponding panel.

39
00:02:14,956 --> 00:02:19,810
An option marked disabled must not become selectable through a separate click handler.

40
00:02:19,810 --> 00:02:23,545
An aria-modal attribute does not itself make the background inert.

41
00:02:24,919 --> 00:02:27,769
Choose native semantics before adding ARIA · 3

42
00:02:28,895 --> 00:02:33,525
Use the WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices patterns as an interaction reference for composite widgets.

43
00:02:33,525 --> 00:02:36,731
They provide expected keyboard behavior and explain focus strategies.

44
00:02:36,731 --> 00:02:39,937
They are guidance rather than a blanket compliance certificate.

45
00:02:39,937 --> 00:02:44,568
Select the pattern that fits the user task, then implement the whole model.

46
00:02:44,568 --> 00:02:47,774
A row of buttons is not automatically a tablist,

47
00:02:47,774 --> 00:02:51,692
and a list of links may be better navigation than a

48
00:02:51,692 --> 00:02:52,405
custom listbox.

49
00:02:53,780 --> 00:02:55,220
Accessible names and descriptions

50
00:02:56,345 --> 00:02:58,569
An accessible name identifies a control.

51
00:02:58,569 --> 00:03:02,647
For a visible text button, its content often supplies that name.

52
00:03:02,647 --> 00:03:07,096
An icon-only button needs another naming source, such as aria-label or aria-labelledby.

53
00:03:07,096 --> 00:03:10,804
Hide decorative icon markup from assistive technology so an SVG

54
00:03:10,804 --> 00:03:14,511
title or glyph does not compete with the intended label.

55
00:03:14,511 --> 00:03:16,736
The label should describe the action,

56
00:03:16,736 --> 00:03:19,702
such as Delete invoice, rather than the image,

57
00:03:19,702 --> 00:03:21,185
such as Trash can.

58
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:24,658
Accessible names and descriptions · 2

59
00:03:25,782 --> 00:03:27,961
An accessible description provides supporting information.

60
00:03:27,961 --> 00:03:32,319
Connect instructions, constraints, and errors with aria-describedby rather than replacing the name.

61
00:03:32,319 --> 00:03:36,313
A tooltip is not a reliable substitute for a required name.

62
00:03:36,313 --> 00:03:40,670
Keyboard users must be able to reveal and dismiss supplemental tooltip content,

63
00:03:40,670 --> 00:03:44,664
and the button should already make sense before the tooltip appears.

64
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:48,463
Accessible names and descriptions · 3

65
00:03:49,588 --> 00:03:53,343
Prefer aria-labelledby when visible text already provides the right

66
00:03:53,343 --> 00:03:56,681
wording, particularly when several labels contribute useful context.

67
00:03:56,681 --> 00:04:00,020
Ensure every referenced identifier exists and is unique.

68
00:04:00,020 --> 00:04:02,941
Generated identifiers must work across multiple instances.

69
00:04:02,941 --> 00:04:08,783
Module counters are convenient in simple browser examples, but server rendering and hydration require

70
00:04:08,783 --> 00:04:14,625
a stable strategy so the client does not produce different relationships from the server.

71
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,373
Focus is a resource with an owner

72
00:04:19,498 --> 00:04:23,226
Focus tells the keyboard user where the next operation will happen.

73
00:04:23,226 --> 00:04:25,938
Keep a visible focus indicator, avoid unnecessary positive

74
00:04:25,938 --> 00:04:28,650
tabindex values, and preserve a logical reading order.

75
00:04:28,650 --> 00:04:32,718
When a component moves focus, it should have a clear user-facing reason.

76
00:04:32,718 --> 00:04:37,464
Opening a modal is one such reason; receiving a background toast normally is not.

77
00:04:38,839 --> 00:04:41,943
Focus is a resource with an owner · 2

78
00:04:43,068 --> 00:04:45,826
Before opening a modal, remember the invoking element.

79
00:04:45,826 --> 00:04:48,929
On open, focus an appropriate element inside the dialog.

80
00:04:48,929 --> 00:04:53,067
Contain interaction while it is modal and provide a keyboard dismissal path.

81
00:04:53,067 --> 00:04:57,549
On close, restore focus when the invoking element still exists and is usable.

82
00:04:57,549 --> 00:04:59,618
If a destructive action removed it,

83
00:04:59,618 --> 00:05:02,721
choose a logical next location rather than focusing a

84
00:05:02,721 --> 00:05:03,411
detached node.

85
00:05:03,411 --> 00:05:05,825
This fallback is a production design responsibility.

86
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:09,972
Focus is a resource with an owner · 3

87
00:05:11,097 --> 00:05:13,311
Initial focus depends on content and risk.

88
00:05:13,311 --> 00:05:16,158
A destructive confirmation commonly starts on the safe action.

89
00:05:16,158 --> 00:05:19,637
A long structured explanation can start at a heading or paragraph

90
00:05:19,637 --> 00:05:23,117
with tabindex minus one so users can read it before acting.

91
00:05:23,117 --> 00:05:27,545
Do not focus the entire dialog element merely because it is easy to query.

92
00:05:27,545 --> 00:05:31,025
Native dialog and the CDK reduce the amount of custom behavior,

93
00:05:31,025 --> 00:05:34,505
but they still need a name and a deliberate focus policy.

94
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:37,553
Modal dialogs and content creation

95
00:05:38,678 --> 00:05:42,022
Use showModal on a native dialog when its behavior fits.

96
00:05:42,022 --> 00:05:44,697
It places the dialog in the top layer

97
00:05:44,697 --> 00:05:47,372
and makes the rest of the document inert.

98
00:05:47,372 --> 00:05:52,054
Merely setting the open attribute creates an open dialog without the same modal operation.

99
00:05:52,054 --> 00:05:54,729
The distinction explains why visually covering a page

100
00:05:54,729 --> 00:05:57,405
does not prevent keyboard interaction with controls underneath.

101
00:05:58,779 --> 00:06:01,222
Modal dialogs and content creation · 2

102
00:06:02,348 --> 00:06:05,965
Keep the Angular state and the native dialog state connected.

103
00:06:05,965 --> 00:06:07,774
Escape produces a cancel event.

104
00:06:07,774 --> 00:06:12,115
A form with method dialog can close the element through another path.

105
00:06:12,115 --> 00:06:16,818
Route those paths back to the component's model, and restore focus after closure.

106
00:06:16,818 --> 00:06:18,989
Invoke browser DOM operations after rendering,

107
00:06:18,989 --> 00:06:21,883
not during a constructor before the view query

108
00:06:21,883 --> 00:06:22,245
exists.

109
00:06:23,620 --> 00:06:26,126
Modal dialogs and content creation · 3

110
00:06:27,251 --> 00:06:29,398
Projection is another source of surprises.

111
00:06:29,398 --> 00:06:32,262
A parent creates its projected content even if

112
00:06:32,262 --> 00:06:34,768
the child's ng-content appears inside a condition.

113
00:06:34,768 --> 00:06:37,632
Hiding a dialog does not prevent an expensive

114
00:06:37,632 --> 00:06:40,495
projected form from being created or making requests.

115
00:06:40,495 --> 00:06:44,075
Offer a template slot that is instantiated only when open,

116
00:06:44,075 --> 00:06:47,655
or a dialog service that creates a component on demand.

117
00:06:47,655 --> 00:06:49,444
This separates visibility from lifetime.

118
00:06:50,820 --> 00:06:52,813
Composite widgets and keyboard tables

119
00:06:53,938 --> 00:06:58,816
A composite widget groups several related items into a coherent keyboard interaction.

120
00:06:58,816 --> 00:07:03,288
A tablist generally has one tab in the page's tab order.

121
00:07:03,288 --> 00:07:06,947
Arrow keys move within it, Home and End reach

122
00:07:06,947 --> 00:07:10,606
the extremes, and selection determines which panel is shown.

123
00:07:10,606 --> 00:07:14,265
Keep roles, aria-selected, tabindex, aria-controls, and panel labeling consistent.

124
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:18,102
Composite widgets and keyboard tables · 2

125
00:07:19,227 --> 00:07:22,040
Automatic activation selects a tab as focus moves.

126
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:24,502
It works well when panels display immediately.

127
00:07:24,502 --> 00:07:27,667
Manual activation moves focus first and selects on Enter

128
00:07:27,667 --> 00:07:30,480
or Space, which can avoid repeated slow requests.

129
00:07:30,480 --> 00:07:32,942
Do not combine half of each model.

130
00:07:32,942 --> 00:07:35,756
If scrolling buttons are added for narrow screens,

131
00:07:35,756 --> 00:07:39,624
preserve keyboard access to the actual tabs and scroll the focused

132
00:07:39,624 --> 00:07:40,679
tab into view.

133
00:07:40,679 --> 00:07:43,845
The extra buttons should not replace the tablist semantics.

134
00:07:45,220 --> 00:07:47,737
Composite widgets and keyboard tables · 3

135
00:07:48,863 --> 00:07:52,819
Track identity rather than assuming a static index is always enough.

136
00:07:52,819 --> 00:07:57,855
Removing the final tab must not leave an inaccessible panel or no reachable tab.

137
00:07:57,855 --> 00:08:02,171
Handle empty lists, disabled items if supported, right-to-left direction, and multiple groups.

138
00:08:02,171 --> 00:08:05,408
A key manager can help with traversal and wrapping,

139
00:08:05,408 --> 00:08:09,005
but it does not supply the entire semantic and selection

140
00:08:09,005 --> 00:08:09,365
contract.

141
00:08:10,740 --> 00:08:12,638
Form relationships and error timing

142
00:08:13,763 --> 00:08:17,559
A visible label needs a programmatic connection to the input.

143
00:08:17,559 --> 00:08:22,114
Match label for with input id, or use valid wrapping label semantics.

144
00:08:22,114 --> 00:08:25,531
A custom component wrapper cannot rely on an identifier

145
00:08:25,531 --> 00:08:28,568
on its host automatically naming an internal input.

146
00:08:28,568 --> 00:08:32,365
Form-field abstractions need an explicit relationship to the actual control.

147
00:08:33,740 --> 00:08:36,058
Form relationships and error timing · 2

148
00:08:37,183 --> 00:08:40,647
Use aria-describedby for helper and error text and aria-invalid when

149
00:08:40,647 --> 00:08:44,112
the control is invalid according to the application's display policy.

150
00:08:44,112 --> 00:08:46,884
Do not announce every validation detail before the

151
00:08:46,884 --> 00:08:49,656
user has had a chance to enter data.

152
00:08:49,656 --> 00:08:52,775
An error shown after blur or submission usually needs

153
00:08:52,775 --> 00:08:55,547
a clear explanation and a route to correction.

154
00:08:55,547 --> 00:08:59,704
Preserve instructions when adding an error rather than dropping their identifier accidentally.

155
00:09:01,080 --> 00:09:03,981
Form relationships and error timing · 3

156
00:09:05,106 --> 00:09:10,907
For live error updates, establish the live region before putting the message into it.

157
00:09:10,907 --> 00:09:15,881
Some assistive technology misses a region inserted together with its first text.

158
00:09:15,881 --> 00:09:18,367
Keep the name, description, visual error,

159
00:09:18,367 --> 00:09:21,683
and live announcement synchronized, but avoid redundant repeated

160
00:09:21,683 --> 00:09:22,097
speech.

161
00:09:22,097 --> 00:09:27,485
Verify the structural relationships automatically and test actual announcements manually on supported combinations.

162
00:09:28,860 --> 00:09:30,602
Live regions are asynchronous communication

163
00:09:31,727 --> 00:09:36,607
A toast's presence on screen does not mean a screen reader will announce it.

164
00:09:36,607 --> 00:09:39,396
A persistent live region provides a communication channel.

165
00:09:39,396 --> 00:09:43,579
Polite announcements usually wait for an appropriate pause; assertive announcements may interrupt.

166
00:09:43,579 --> 00:09:48,110
Choose urgency according to the user impact, not the color of the toast.

167
00:09:48,110 --> 00:09:51,945
Routine success feedback should rarely interrupt what the user is reading.

168
00:09:53,320 --> 00:09:55,769
Live regions are asynchronous communication · 2

169
00:09:56,894 --> 00:10:00,042
Separate the visible notification history from the announcement channel.

170
00:10:00,042 --> 00:10:03,191
Replacing a list of toasts can cause too much

171
00:10:03,191 --> 00:10:06,339
content to be spoken or can repeat old messages.

172
00:10:06,339 --> 00:10:09,838
Publish the specific new status through a live announcer or

173
00:10:09,838 --> 00:10:13,336
persistent region while leaving the visible items available for review.

174
00:10:13,336 --> 00:10:17,885
Do not move focus into a noninteractive toast simply to make it noticeable.

175
00:10:19,260 --> 00:10:21,765
Live regions are asynchronous communication · 3

176
00:10:22,890 --> 00:10:26,945
Rapid changes can be coalesced or interrupted depending on the platform.

177
00:10:26,945 --> 00:10:31,001
A sequence such as three files uploaded, then four files uploaded,

178
00:10:31,001 --> 00:10:34,688
is not a reliable promise of two separate spoken sentences.

179
00:10:34,688 --> 00:10:38,744
Batch progress, announce a final summary, and offer stable visible status.

180
00:10:38,744 --> 00:10:43,169
Tests can verify that the intended text reaches a connected live region;

181
00:10:43,169 --> 00:10:47,225
they cannot assert a universal spoken transcript from a DOM change.

182
00:10:48,600 --> 00:10:50,664
Listbox focus and selection are different

183
00:10:51,789 --> 00:10:54,885
A single-select listbox lets a user choose one option.

184
00:10:54,885 --> 00:10:58,325
The active option is the one keyboard navigation currently addresses.

185
00:10:58,325 --> 00:11:00,733
The selected value is the committed choice.

186
00:11:00,733 --> 00:11:03,485
These can differ when arrows move without selecting.

187
00:11:03,485 --> 00:11:07,957
Pick a documented behavior and keep it consistent for pointer and keyboard interaction.

188
00:11:07,957 --> 00:11:12,085
A native select is often preferable when it fits the product need.

189
00:11:13,460 --> 00:11:16,171
Listbox focus and selection are different · 2

190
00:11:17,296 --> 00:11:20,546
With aria-activedescendant, DOM focus stays on the listbox container.

191
00:11:20,546 --> 00:11:23,435
The attribute points to the rendered active option,

192
00:11:23,435 --> 00:11:25,963
which must exist and have an identifier.

193
00:11:25,963 --> 00:11:31,018
Roving tabindex is an alternative for a standalone widget: focus moves among option elements.

194
00:11:31,018 --> 00:11:33,185
Do not mix both strategies casually.

195
00:11:33,185 --> 00:11:36,435
Neither strategy removes the need to scroll the active

196
00:11:36,435 --> 00:11:39,325
option into view and display a visible indicator.

197
00:11:40,700 --> 00:11:43,876
Listbox focus and selection are different · 3

198
00:11:45,001 --> 00:11:48,644
Use roles listbox and option, an accessible name on

199
00:11:48,644 --> 00:11:52,288
the container, aria-selected on options, and aria-disabled where appropriate.

200
00:11:52,288 --> 00:11:55,932
Disabled options remain discoverable but must not be chosen.

201
00:11:55,932 --> 00:11:57,957
Keep identifiers unique across instances.

202
00:11:57,957 --> 00:12:00,386
In an empty or all-disabled list,

203
00:12:00,386 --> 00:12:04,030
remove an invalid active descendant reference and let navigation

204
00:12:04,030 --> 00:12:05,244
safely do nothing.

205
00:12:06,620 --> 00:12:08,548
Listbox state, typeahead, and dynamic data

206
00:12:09,673 --> 00:12:12,610
The coding exercise uses a model for the selected

207
00:12:12,610 --> 00:12:15,548
value and a linked signal for the active index.

208
00:12:15,548 --> 00:12:19,139
When options change, reconcile the active item against current data instead

209
00:12:19,139 --> 00:12:22,404
of keeping an index that can point past the list.

210
00:12:22,404 --> 00:12:25,015
Object values may need compareWith because a refetch

211
00:12:25,015 --> 00:12:27,300
can return equal records with different references.

212
00:12:27,300 --> 00:12:30,564
Do not mutate the consumer's options to store local selection.

213
00:12:31,940 --> 00:12:34,952
Listbox state, typeahead, and dynamic data · 2

214
00:12:36,077 --> 00:12:39,464
Arrow keys, Home, and End traverse enabled options; Enter

215
00:12:39,464 --> 00:12:42,852
and Space commit the active item in this example.

216
00:12:42,852 --> 00:12:46,240
Prevent the browser's scrolling default for handled navigation keys.

217
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:51,510
Printable characters build a short typeahead buffer, with a reset timer and modifier-key filtering.

218
00:12:51,510 --> 00:12:53,392
Clear the timer on destruction.

219
00:12:53,392 --> 00:12:56,780
Repeated-letter cycling deserves special attention: a buffer that becomes

220
00:12:56,780 --> 00:12:59,791
two identical letters does not automatically implement cycling.

221
00:12:59,791 --> 00:13:04,684
We will document that limitation in the source example and show a correction.

222
00:13:06,059 --> 00:13:09,113
Listbox state, typeahead, and dynamic data · 3

223
00:13:10,239 --> 00:13:12,213
Virtualization adds an important constraint.

224
00:13:12,213 --> 00:13:16,952
An active descendant cannot safely reference an option removed from the DOM.

225
00:13:16,952 --> 00:13:22,086
A large list needs an identity and rendering strategy that keeps the active

226
00:13:22,086 --> 00:13:26,826
option represented, correct position and set-size metadata where required, and integration tests.

227
00:13:26,826 --> 00:13:29,985
Do not add virtual scrolling to a semantic

228
00:13:29,985 --> 00:13:33,144
listbox and assume the accessibility contract survives unchanged.

229
00:13:34,520 --> 00:13:36,723
Style from state and verify transitions

230
00:13:37,848 --> 00:13:40,137
Selection should remain recognizable without color.

231
00:13:40,137 --> 00:13:44,333
A check mark or another shape can supplement background and weight.

232
00:13:44,333 --> 00:13:47,767
Style from ARIA state where it reflects the actual

233
00:13:47,767 --> 00:13:51,200
behavioral state, so a separate CSS flag cannot drift.

234
00:13:51,200 --> 00:13:54,252
Use design tokens for focus rings, surfaces, spacing,

235
00:13:54,252 --> 00:13:57,305
and disabled colors, with forced-colors adjustments where necessary.

236
00:13:58,679 --> 00:14:01,843
Style from state and verify transitions · 2

237
00:14:02,968 --> 00:14:06,921
Build tests around transitions: focus enters, arrows move, selection commits,

238
00:14:06,921 --> 00:14:09,689
options change, a disabled item is skipped,

239
00:14:09,689 --> 00:14:11,666
and focus returns after dismissal.

240
00:14:11,666 --> 00:14:14,434
Render two instances to catch duplicate identifiers.

241
00:14:14,434 --> 00:14:16,411
Test empty and all-disabled data.

242
00:14:16,411 --> 00:14:21,155
For dialogs, use real Tab and Escape events in browser automation because

243
00:14:21,155 --> 00:14:25,504
synthetic dispatch alone does not perform the browser's default focus behavior.

244
00:14:26,880 --> 00:14:29,832
Style from state and verify transitions · 3

245
00:14:30,958 --> 00:14:35,017
An automated accessibility scanner catches many missing names and invalid relationships,

246
00:14:35,017 --> 00:14:38,708
but it does not prove the widget's full interaction pattern.

247
00:14:38,708 --> 00:14:41,660
A test can pass every scanner rule and

248
00:14:41,660 --> 00:14:44,243
still fail to move focus with ArrowRight.

249
00:14:44,243 --> 00:14:48,303
Combine structural checks, behavior tests, visual review, and manual assistive-technology checks.

250
00:14:48,303 --> 00:14:51,625
We will use this layered method in every walkthrough.

