A11Y-001 · Interview question

277
00:16:24,479 --> 00:16:26,918
List the accessibility failures, fix the component,

278
00:16:26,918 --> 00:16:30,750
and explain how you would release the fix to hundreds of

279
00:16:30,750 --> 00:16:31,796
existing call sites.

280
00:16:38,446 --> 00:16:39,702
A11Y-001 · 1.

281
00:16:39,702 --> 00:16:41,796
It is not a button

282
00:16:42,921 --> 00:16:46,101
A <div> with a click handler has no role,

283
00:16:46,101 --> 00:16:50,341
is not in the tab order, and does not respond to Enter

284
00:16:50,341 --> 00:16:51,047
or Space.

285
00:16:51,047 --> 00:16:55,641
Keyboard users cannot reach it; screen readers do not announce it as interactive.

286
00:16:55,641 --> 00:17:00,588
Adding role="button", tabindex="0" and key handlers is possible but recreates what the native element

287
00:17:00,588 --> 00:17:05,535
already does, usually incompletely (Space activates on key up for native buttons, for example).

288
00:17:06,910 --> 00:17:08,168
A11Y-001 · 1.

289
00:17:08,168 --> 00:17:10,265
It is not a button

290
00:17:11,390 --> 00:17:14,912
Fix: use a native <button type="button">. type="button" matters:

291
00:17:14,912 --> 00:17:18,435
a button inside a <form> defaults to submit.

292
00:17:19,810 --> 00:17:20,967
A11Y-001 · 2.

293
00:17:20,967 --> 00:17:23,669
The accessible name is the ligature text

294
00:17:24,795 --> 00:17:28,739
The only text inside is more_vert, so assistive technology announces something like

295
00:17:28,739 --> 00:17:32,683
"more underscore vert". title on the custom element does not help: it

296
00:17:32,683 --> 00:17:35,313
is on a host element with no role,

297
00:17:35,313 --> 00:17:39,257
and title is not reliably exposed or shown to keyboard and touch

298
00:17:39,257 --> 00:17:39,914
users anyway.

299
00:17:41,290 --> 00:17:42,536
A11Y-001 · 2.

300
00:17:42,536 --> 00:17:45,446
The accessible name is the ligature text

301
00:17:46,571 --> 00:17:50,190
Fix: a label input bound to aria-label on the button,

302
00:17:50,190 --> 00:17:54,172
and aria-hidden="true" on the icon so the ligature text is never

303
00:17:54,172 --> 00:17:54,534
read.

304
00:17:55,910 --> 00:17:57,273
A11Y-001 · 3.

305
00:17:57,273 --> 00:17:59,092
Disabled is visual only

306
00:18:00,217 --> 00:18:05,166
The class greys the button out, but nothing tells assistive technology it is unavailable.

307
00:18:05,166 --> 00:18:09,055
There are two valid fixes, and the choice should be deliberate:

308
00:18:10,430 --> 00:18:11,917
A11Y-001 · 3.

309
00:18:11,917 --> 00:18:13,900
Disabled is visual only

310
00:18:15,025 --> 00:18:18,667
Native disabled: removes the button from the tab order.

311
00:18:18,667 --> 00:18:23,524
Simple, but users cannot discover the control or a tooltip explaining why

312
00:18:23,524 --> 00:18:28,381
it is disabled. - aria-disabled="true": keeps it focusable and announced as unavailable.

313
00:18:28,381 --> 00:18:31,215
The component must then block activation itself.

314
00:18:32,590 --> 00:18:34,224
A11Y-001 · 3.

315
00:18:34,224 --> 00:18:36,403
Disabled is visual only

316
00:18:37,528 --> 00:18:43,035
The fixed version uses aria-disabled so that disabled actions in toolbars remain discoverable.

