01:00:03,035 --> 01:00:06,145
A11Y-005 · What a strong candidate also mentions

992
01:00:07,270 --> 01:00:10,839
The CDK LiveAnnouncer already manages a hidden live region and can

993
01:00:10,839 --> 01:00:14,408
be used instead of the visible regions for the announcement itself.

994
01:00:14,408 --> 01:00:17,329
Focus is lost if a focused toast is dismissed.

995
01:00:17,329 --> 01:00:20,249
When the user dismisses a toast they tabbed into,

996
01:00:20,249 --> 01:00:23,819
focus should go somewhere sensible, such as back to where it

997
01:00:23,819 --> 01:00:24,468
was before.

998
01:00:24,468 --> 01:00:26,739
Stacking: five toasts at once are unreadable.

999
01:00:26,739 --> 01:00:29,659
Limit how many are visible and queue the rest.

1000
01:00:31,035 --> 01:00:33,170
A11Y-005 · Follow-up 1

1001
01:00:34,295 --> 01:00:39,496
A toast says "3 files uploaded" and then "4 files uploaded" a second later.

1002
01:00:39,496 --> 01:00:43,955
What will a screen reader say, and how would you improve it?

1003
01:00:49,069 --> 01:00:52,271
Speech may skip, merge, or interrupt rapid updates; do

1004
01:00:52,271 --> 01:00:55,118
not promise that both counts are read exactly.

1005
01:00:55,118 --> 01:00:57,965
Batch progress updates and announce a useful final

1006
01:00:57,965 --> 01:01:00,456
summary, while keeping current visible status available.

1007
01:01:00,456 --> 01:01:03,303
A live announcer can replace an outdated progress

1008
01:01:03,303 --> 01:01:05,794
message rather than queueing every intermediate number.

1009
01:01:05,794 --> 01:01:08,286
Avoid repeatedly speaking the entire toast history.

1010
01:01:08,286 --> 01:01:11,133
Test the message sequence and manually evaluate the

1011
01:01:11,133 --> 01:01:13,980
supported assistive technology to choose a useful cadence.

1012
01:01:15,355 --> 01:01:17,550
A11Y-005 · Follow-up 2

1013
01:01:18,675 --> 01:01:21,269
Should the outlet be a landmark region?

1014
01:01:21,269 --> 01:01:22,752
What are the trade-offs?

1015
01:01:28,202 --> 01:01:31,496
A named region can make a persistent notification history

1016
01:01:31,496 --> 01:01:34,790
discoverable, especially if it contains actions users may revisit.

1017
01:01:34,790 --> 01:01:38,816
But turning every transient toast into a landmark creates navigation noise.

1018
01:01:38,816 --> 01:01:42,842
Prefer one meaningful named outlet when it serves a navigable purpose,

1019
01:01:42,842 --> 01:01:46,501
and use a separate status announcement channel for new messages.

1020
01:01:46,501 --> 01:01:50,893
Do not move focus to the region merely because a toast appeared.

1021
01:01:50,893 --> 01:01:54,553
Evaluate the page's existing landmarks and the user's recovery task.

1022
01:01:55,929 --> 01:01:58,054
